**Explanation**

The game is a variant of the *fire‑fighter problem* on the infinite square grid.  
A flooded cell spreads each turn to all orthogonal neighbours that are not
separated from it by a wall.  Sun Wukong may place walls, but after his
\(n\)‑th turn the total length of the barrier may not exceed \(\gamma n\).
Hence after turn \(n\) he can have placed at most  

\[
L_n=\bigl\lfloor\gamma n\bigr\rfloor
\]

unit edges, i.e. on turn \(n\) he can add  

\[
\Delta_n=L_n-L_{n-1}\in\{\lfloor\gamma\rfloor,\;\lfloor\gamma\rfloor+1\}
\]

new edges.  When \(\gamma>2\) we have \(\lfloor\gamma\rfloor\ge 2\); when
\(1\le\gamma<2\) we have \(\lfloor\gamma\rfloor=1\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Upper bound – \(\gamma>2\) is sufficient

Assume \(\gamma>2\).  Then on every turn Sun Wukong can add at least two
edges (sometimes three, but two are enough).  He uses them to build a
straight *fire‑break* line that moves outward one cell per turn.

*Construction.*  
Choose a column, say column \(x=0\).  In the first turn place the two
vertical edges \(((0,0),(0,1))\) and \(((0,1),(0,2))\).  The line now blocks
the flood from crossing the \(x=0\) line above row 2.  In each subsequent
turn add the two vertical edges that extend the line one cell higher:
\(((0,k),(0,k+1))\) and \(((0,k+1),(0,k+2))\).  Because Sun Wukong can add at
least two edges each turn, the line can be extended indefinitely.

The flood spreads at speed 1 in the Manhattan metric, while the fire‑break
advances upward at the same speed.  Consequently the flooded region is
confined to the half‑plane \(x<0\).  After finitely many turns Sun Wukong can
close the line at the top by adding a horizontal segment of length at most
\(\gamma\) (possible because \(\gamma>2\)).  The resulting closed loop
encloses all flooded cells, so Sun Wukong wins.

Thus for every \(\gamma>2\) Sun Wukong has a winning strategy.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Lower bound – \(\gamma\le 2\) is not enough

Now let \(1\le\gamma\le2\).  Then \(\lfloor\gamma\rfloor=1\); on most turns Sun
Wukong can add only **one** edge, and only on a fraction \(\{\gamma\}\) of the
turns can he add a second edge.  Over \(t\) turns he can place at most  

\[
\bigl\lfloor\gamma t\bigr\rfloor\le 2t-1
\]

edges, i.e. fewer than two edges per turn on average.

The Sea God starts with a single flooded cell at the origin.  After \(k\)
turns the set of flooded cells is the Manhattan ball  

\[
B_k=\{(x,y)\mid |x|+|y|\le k\},
\]

which has perimeter \(4k\).  To prevent the fire from escaping a
half‑plane, Sun Wukong would have to extend a fire‑break line by one cell
each turn, which requires **two** new edges per turn.  Because his average
budget is \(<2\) edges per turn, after sufficiently many turns the fire’s
perimeter exceeds the total number of edges he could have placed, so the
fire inevitably reaches the other side of any attempted barrier.  Hence the
Sea God can keep the flood uncontained.

Consequently, for any \(\gamma\le2\) there exists a strategy for the Sea
God that defeats Sun Wukong.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Determination of the critical constant

From the two parts we have

* Sun Wukong can guarantee victory for every \(\gamma>2\);
* there are values \(\gamma\le2\) for which he cannot guarantee victory.

Therefore the supremum of the set of \(\gamma\) for which a guarantee may
fail is \(2\).  This is the largest constant \(C\) such that “for all
\(\gamma>C\) Sun Wukong can guarantee victory’’ holds.

\[
\boxed{C=2}
\]

